ollars in  cash on hand  four republicans have filed to run  against her april becker who  narrowly lost a state senate race last  year has raised the most money she's  raised 143 thousand dollars and only  spent about 10 grand so far  mark robertson has raised 69 thousand  dollars  up next congressman stephen horsford in  congressional district four he has  raised  about five hundred seventy thousand  dollars he says he has one point two  million dollars on hand right now  republican challenger sam peters has  raised the most from the republican side  spent about twenty thousand dollars of  it so far carolina serrano has also  declared that she plans to run but she  hasn't filed any paperwork yet that  we've found  on the fec website so far the most high  profile race that will be for  senate senator catherine cortez masto up  for re-election  she has raised about 2.8 million dollars  so far  she has more than six million on hand  for the campaign two republicans have  filed to run against her although  neither one of us posted any fundraising  reports just yet  one is nevada 2020 a winner  shirelle mendenhall she owns a talent  agency here in town and describes  herself as a  strong christian and lifelong  conservative second is army veteran  named sam brown he was injured in  afghanistan and released his first ad  already  if you're ready to put others first if  you're ready to take back control and  demand accountability from a political  class  that has failed to serve the  constitution then i hope you'll join our  movement  i'm sam brown and i'm running for u.s  senate  so far they are the only two people to  actually file to run former attorney  general adam laxalt is also testing the  waters  as for the work on capitol hill senator  cortez masto and congresswoman susie lee  they've introduced a bill to try and  deal with the drought on the colorado  river  this new measure opens up federal  funding to address mitigation and water  saving plans  i spoke with the senator about her her  bill  so the legislation that i'm introducing  um and  will be working on as part of the  package tomorrow in enr  energy and natural resources focuses on  large-scale water recycling projects  uh what is that that means you're taking  uh water like sewage water and recycling  it into drinking water  now nevada does a really good job and  already does that but there are several  states along with colorado that do not  so the legislation that i have  incentivizes them to engage in these  large-scale  projects with the benefit of a grant  program through the department of  interior  and the legislation is important  obviously because the more water that we  can  new water that we can really put back  into the colorado  uh helps nevada and so many other states  is there  in your view a willingness on farmers on  other states organizations upstream from  nevada  to want to take advantage of a grant  program like this  absolutely and i that that's the goal  here is listen we are all  the states that border the the colorado  and take water from the colorado of all  the states we all have to work  well together and we do the  collaboration is so  important for us uh particularly  as we all seek the water off the  colorado for  our communities and that that that is a  working relationship  it requires the federal government to  work with us the department of the  interior or overseas or is the  the keeper of the law of the river um  and the bureau of reclamation which is  just as important for the hydropower  that comes  from the colorado river as well so yes  there is a collaboration and we've seen  it in nevada the conservation that can  happen when  community members everybody comes  together to recognize that  really drought is our norm and we have  to plan for that we have to understand  it  and we have to conserve our water and we  have to make sure that we are  conserving it and preserving it for  future as well  now that bill did pass the first hurdle  in capitol hill and made it out of its  first committee  this week we'll keep you posted next on  politics now are changes coming in cuba  we'll have a look at the protests  scripting that country and how cubans  living here in las vegas are showing  their solidarity  you're watching politics now  recently protesters have been filling  the streets of cuba rallying against the  communist government there  the cuban protests have been triggered  by food shortages rising prices and the  government's response to  more covid19 infections well on tuesday  hundreds of people marched in solidarity  right here  in las vegas i think it's very important  for our voice to be heard  since we are such a big country the more  we go out  the better it is if cuba is on the  streets  we can be on the streets as well we need  everybody to open their eyes  because the reality is that  we're hurting a lot protesters are also  calling on the u.s government to  intervene  on monday president biden released a  statement saying the united states calls  on the cuban regime to hear their people  and serve their needs at this vital  moment  the homeland security secretary says the  u.s will not give any additional help  to folks fleeing cuba by boat in  washington the biden administration is  signing more people to work on the  backlog of daca applicants the dreamers  since the program reopened in december  there were more than 81  000 new applicants only 1900 have been  processed  another 13 thousand renewal requests are  waiting  senator cortez masto's been one of those  on capitol hill  pressing the administration to deal with  this backlog  senate democrats have unveiled a draft  bill to decriminalize marijuana  nationwide  it would remove pot from the federal  controlled substances list  give cannabis businesses access to  banking it regulates and taxes marijuana  sales and clears records for  non-violent marijuana crimes there is  some pushback but democratic leaders say  federal pot laws disproportionately hurt  minorities  in 2019 there were more marijuana  arrests  than all other violent crime arrests  combined  and the majority of those were for  simple possession it's invested in by  tobacco alcohol and pharma  the drug is much more potent and much  more addictive than it's ever  been talk about this alex limon from our  washington dc bureau alex  here in nevada we've had recreational  marijuana for four years  and a problem we've heard from the very  beginning banks won't work with them  so how does this help solve that well  that's right right now banks cannot work  with marijuana related businesses  because  marijuana is still illegal on a federal  level both sales  as well as possession but what this  proposal from the top  some of the top democrats in the senate  would do  is remove marijuana from the federal  controlled substances list  in essence decriminalizing it and taking  away  also a lot of the enforcement because it  would actually shift  the enforcement of marijuana related  matters from the drug enforcement agency  that handles it now to the food and drug  administration and so the idea  is that instead of criminalizing it  the the government could instead  regulate and even tax marijuana sales so  it would be a complete  game changer for those banks to be able  to work with dispensaries and grow  operations and other related businesses  what potentially would be sort of wiped  clean well so what the authors of this  bill  want is for anyone who has  low level non-violent marijuana related  convictions like simple possession to be  able to expunge their records  and also for people who are still  currently serving time to be able to  petition  to have their sentences reduced again  for those similar low-level  non-violent type of offenses all right  so what's the reaction on capitol hill  especially from republicans in  on the other side of the aisle any idea  that this isn't even a chance of passing  democrats would have to get 10  republicans along with every single  democrat in the u.s senate to agree to  this proposal in order to make it law  that's probably  going to be pretty difficult there are  obviously many conservative  republicans who feel that marijuana  should not be  decriminalized and some who even feel  that this bill  in essence legalizes it while it doesn't  force states to allow the sale of  recreational marijuana again you're  taking away that federal enforcement arm  so they say in essence it does legalize  it  however senator chuck schumer is trying  to remain positive he said that if  cities and states very conservative  states like south dakota  can in fact pass decriminalization laws  then he feels that it should be possible  on a federal level as well and he said  he's going to put his clout and his  weight as a majority leader  to try to get this initiative through  the senate  and that was alex limon reporting from  washington d.c  next on politics now trying for  diversity in pot ownership  we're going to talk about the meeting  that figures out rules for pot lounges  moving forward  this week in what to watch how will pot  lounges how will those licenses be  handed out moving forward  on monday there will be a meeting held  with a cannabis equity and inclusion  community to try and talk about it  one of the requirements is half of the  licenses would be handed out to  social equity applicants this meeting  will figure out  exactly what that means and who counts  as a social equity applicant of course  the pot lounges approved  in the last legislative session we'll  have that for you coming up next week as  well  also we're going to keep following the  changes with the coronavirus regulations  the state saying  that possibly folks should be wearing  masks even if you're vaccinated inside a  recommendation  we'll continue to follow those changes  as well moving forward with that we  thank you very much for watching  politics now for the latest in